/**
 * \defgroup incomplete_beta Regularized incomplete beta function
 *
 * The incomplete beta module evaluates the regularized incomplete beta function, its
 * complement, their natural logarithms, mixed partial derivatives with respect to both shape
 * parameters up to order \f$6\f$, and their scalar and log-probability inverses on host and
 * device (`LAPLACE_HD`). All routines are declared across
 * `<laplace/math/incomplete_beta.hpp>`, `<laplace/math/incomplete_beta_deriv.hpp>`, and
 * `<laplace/math/incomplete_inverse.hpp>` in namespace `laplace::math` (with bundled result
 * structures and pair-returning inverses in `laplace::math::own`). Values agree with arbitrary-precision
 * references within a few ULPs across standard, large-shape, and deep-tail inputs.
 *
 * ## Definitions
 *
 * For positive shape parameters \f$a, b > 0\f$ and argument \f$x \in [0, 1]\f$, the
 * regularized incomplete beta function (DLMF §8.17) is
 *
 * \f[
 * I_x(a, b) = \frac{1}{B(a, b)} \int_0^x t^{a-1} (1 - t)^{b-1} \, \mathrm{d}t,
 * \qquad
 * B(a, b) = \frac{\Gamma(a)\,\Gamma(b)}{\Gamma(a + b)}.
 * \f]
 *
 * Its complement satisfies \f$1 - I_x(a, b) = I_{1-x}(b, a)\f$. Calling
 * \ref laplace::math::own::beta_inc "beta_inc()" evaluates \f$I_x(a, b)\f$, \f$1 - I_x(a, b)\f$,
 * \f$\log I_x(a, b)\f$, \f$\log(1 - I_x(a, b))\f$, and the double-double low-order log parts
 * `log_i_lo` and `log_ic_lo` in a single \ref laplace::math::own::beta_inc_result "beta_inc_result"
 * structure. Each scalar function — `ibeta()`, `ibetac()`, `log_ibeta()`, and `log_ibetac()` —
 * calls \ref laplace::math::own::beta_inc "beta_inc()" and keeps one field, so evaluating all
 * four together costs no more than one scalar call.
 *
 * The derivative functions `log_ibeta_d1()` through `log_ibeta_d6()` and `log_ibetac_d1()`
 * through `log_ibetac_d6()` compute all mixed partial derivatives of order \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$
 * with respect to \f$(a, b)\f$. They return a
 * \ref laplace::math::own::mixed_partials "mixed_partials<K>" value whose entry `c[j]` stores
 * \f$\partial^K / \partial a^{K-j} \partial b^j\f$ for \f$j = 0, \dots, K\f$. Subtracting the
 * digamma and polygamma derivatives of \f$\log B(a, b)\f$ from the unregularized log-integral
 * cumulants \f$\kappa_{i, j}\f$ yields the derivatives of \f$\log I_x(a, b)\f$:
 *
 * \f[
 * \frac{\partial^K}{\partial a^{K-j} \partial b^j} \log I_x(a, b)
 * = \kappa_{K-j, j} - \frac{\partial^K}{\partial a^{K-j} \partial b^j} \log B(a, b).
 * \f]
 *
 * For the complement, `log_ibetac_dK()` differentiates \f$\log(1 - I_x(a, b))\f$ directly via
 * Faà di Bruno's rule (Johnson, 2002) when \f$I_x(a, b) \le 1/2\f$, and reflects to \f$I_{1-x}(b, a)\f$ when
 * \f$I_x(a, b) > 1/2\f$.
 *
 * The inverse functions `ibeta_inv()` and `ibetac_inv()` solve \f$I_x(a, b) = p\f$ and
 * \f$1 - I_x(a, b) = q\f$ for \f$x\f$. Log-probability variants `ibeta_inv_logp()` and
 * `ibetac_inv_logq()` solve \f$\log I_x(a, b) = \log p\f$ and \f$\log(1 - I_x(a, b)) = \log q\f$
 * for arbitrarily deep tails. Overloads in `laplace::math::own` return a
 * \ref laplace::math::own::beta_inv_result "beta_inv_result" pair holding both \f$x\f$ and
 * \f$1 - x\f$, whereas wrappers in `laplace::math` return \f$x\f$ alone.
 *
 * ## Domain and special values
 *
 * All functions require finite positive shape parameters \f$a, b \in (0, \infty)\f$. Values and
 * logarithms accept \f$x \in [0, 1]\f$; at \f$x = 0\f$ they return \f$I_x = 0\f$,
 * \f$1 - I_x = 1\f$, \f$\log I_x = -\infty\f$, and \f$\log(1 - I_x) = 0\f$, and at \f$x = 1\f$
 * those limits reverse. Shape derivatives of \f$\log I_x(a, b)\f$ are defined on \f$x \in (0, 1]\f$,
 * returning zeros at \f$x = 1\f$ and quiet NaN at \f$x = 0\f$; symmetrically, derivatives of
 * \f$\log(1 - I_x(a, b))\f$ are defined on \f$x \in [0, 1)\f$, returning zeros at \f$x = 0\f$
 * and quiet NaN at \f$x = 1\f$. Out-of-domain arguments, non-positive shapes, infinite shapes for
 * derivatives, or any NaN input return quiet NaN, with low-order log corrections in
 * `beta_inc_result` set to zero.
 *
 * Probability inverses accept \f$p, q \in [0, 1]\f$, while log-probability inverses accept
 * \f$\log p, \log q \in [-\infty, 0]\f$. At \f$p = 0\f$ or \f$\log p = -\infty\f$, `ibeta_inv()`
 * and `ibeta_inv_logp()` return \f$x = 0\f$ and \f$1 - x = 1\f$; at \f$p = 1\f$ or \f$\log p = 0\f$
 * they return \f$x = 1\f$ and \f$1 - x = 0\f$, with the opposite endpoints for `ibetac_inv()` and
 * `ibetac_inv_logq()`. When an interior root rounds below \f$2^{-1075}\f$ in \f$x\f$ it flushes
 * to `{0.0, 1.0}`, and when \f$1 - x\f$ falls at or below \f$2^{-1074}\f$ it flushes to
 * `{1.0, 0.0}`. Invalid shapes, out-of-range targets, infinite shapes, or NaN return `{NaN, NaN}`.
 *
 * ## Method
 *
 * ### Value and logarithms
 *
 * Evaluation always targets the smaller side using \f$\lambda = a - (a + b)x\f$ and the
 * reflection \f$I_x(a, b) = 1 - I_{1-x}(b, a)\f$, preventing subtraction cancellation near unity
 * and preserving relative accuracy in deep tails. The DiDonato and Morris (1992) branch selector
 * chooses among five expansions by regime: a power series near zero when \f$x \le 0.5\f$
 * with \f$b \le 1\f$ or \f$b x \le 0.7\f$ (extending to \f$x \le 0.8\f$ when \f$b \le 1\f$); an upward
 * integer-step recurrence combined with \ref laplace::math::own::gamma_inc "gamma_inc()" when
 * \f$a, b > 1\f$ and \f$b < 40\f$ outside the continued-fraction domain; a finite difference
 * recurrence when \f$a < 500\f$; a continued fraction (DLMF §8.17) otherwise; and a uniform asymptotic
 * expansion (DLMF §8.17) when \f$\min(a, b) > 100\f$ and \f$\lambda \le 0.03\min(a, b)\f$. Every power
 * prefactor \f$x^a (1 - x)^b / B(a, b)\f$, series sum, and logarithmic split propagates
 * double-double precision (Dekker, 1971) before exponentiation so linear and logarithmic outputs remain free of
 * prefactor cancellation.
 *
 * ### Shape derivatives
 *
 * Shape derivatives accumulate raw moments of \f$\log t\f$ and \f$\log(1 - t)\f$ over the
 * unregularized integral and convert them to bivariate cumulants \f$\kappa_{i, j}\f$ by
 * recurrence, avoiding catastrophic cancellation when \f$x\f$ is small or \f$a, b\f$ are large.
 * Inside the power-series region (\f$x \le 0.5\f$ with \f$b \le 1\f$ or \f$b x \le 0.7\f$,
 * extending to \f$x \le 0.8\f$ when \f$b \le 1\f$), series coefficients are differentiated
 * term by term. Elsewhere the integrand is normalized at \f$t = x\f$ and integrated by a
 * Takahasi–Mori (1974) double-exponential rule along rays on the device and host, switching to
 * Gauss–Laguerre quadrature (Golub and Welsch, 1969) on the host when \f$x = 1\f$ and splitting at the interior mode when
 * needed.
 *
 * ### Inverses
 *
 * Changing variables to \f$v = \log(x / (1 - x))\f$ renders both \f$\log I_x(a, b) - \log p\f$
 * and \f$\log(1 - I_x(a, b)) - \log q\f$ strictly concave on \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ for all
 * \f$a, b > 0\f$. All four inverse entry points share one solver that evaluates the log objective
 * and closed-form derivatives up to order three in \f$v\f$ from the beta density and
 * double-double log residuals. From explicit domain and power-law brackets and an asymptotic seed
 * (beta-to-gamma, normal, Wilson–Hilferty (1931), or mode-centered power law), safeguarded Halley steps (Gander, 1985)
 * converge inside the bracket until the relative step falls below \f$\varepsilon_{\mathrm{mach}} / 16\f$,
 * within \f$0.5\,\mathrm{ulp}(x)\f$ or \f$4\varepsilon_{\mathrm{mach}}\f$ on \f$1 - x\f$, and
 * the residual is below \f$10^{-6}\f$ within at most \f$64\f$ steps.
 *
 * ## Accuracy
 *
 * Error metrics follow \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" (\ref accuracy_ulp1 "ulp1",
 * \ref accuracy_ulpL "ulpL", \ref accuracy_mixed "ulpN", and \ref accuracy_inverse "ulpC"). Across
 * general grids, mean plain-ULP errors are \f$9.8\f$ for `ibeta()`, \f$8.2\f$ for `ibetac()`,
 * \f$5.4\f$ for `log_ibeta()`, and \f$3.9\f$ for `log_ibetac()`, whereas Boost.Math errs by up
 * to \f$7.5 \times 10^{15}\f$ ulp on edge cases where Laplace matches MPFR (Fousse and others, 2007).
 *
 * | Function | Suite | gcc max | CUDA max | Test bound |
 * | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
 * | `ibeta()` | general | 2.82 ulp1 / 12.7 ulpL | 3.80 ulp1 / 12.9 ulpL | 5.7 ulp1 / 19.5 ulpL |
 * | `ibeta()` | hard | 3.65 ulp1 / 18.8 ulpL | — | 5.5 ulp1 / 29.5 ulpL |
 * | `ibetac()` | general | 2.82 ulp1 / 16.9 ulpL | 3.80 ulp1 / 12.2 ulpL | 5.7 ulp1 / 25.5 ulpL |
 * | `ibetac()` | hard | 3.65 ulp1 / 18.4 ulpL | — | 5.5 ulp1 / 29.5 ulpL |
 * | `log_ibeta()` | general | 17 ulpL | 12 ulpL | 33.0 ulpL |
 * | `log_ibeta()` | hard | 22 ulpL | 19.7 ulpL | 33.0 ulpL |
 * | `log_ibetac()` | general | 10 ulpL | 15 ulpL | 33.0 ulpL |
 * | `log_ibetac()` | hard | 15 ulpL | 16 ulpL | 33.0 ulpL |
 *
 * For shape derivatives, CUDA results match gcc within a factor of \f$1.9\f$, a norm unit
 * \f$1\,\mathrm{ulp}_N\f$ equals \f$4 \times 10^{-13}\f$, and relative errors stay near
 * \f$10^3\f$ ulp down to \f$I_x \approx 10^{-290}\f$.
 *
 * | Order | gcc max (ulpN) | `log_ibeta_dK` bound (ulpN / ulpL) | `log_ibetac_dK` bound (ulpN / ulpL) |
 * | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
 * | 1 | 1580 | 1600.0 / 55.0 | 2400.0 / 180.0 |
 * | 2 | 1340 | 1800.0 / 245.0 | 2050.0 / 155.0 |
 * | 3 | 1620 | 3100.0 / 315.0 | 2450.0 / 80.0 |
 * | 4 | 1370 | 1800.0 / 115.0 | 2950.0 / 75.0 |
 * | 5 | 1950 | 3300.0 / 420.0 | 2950.0 / 690.0 |
 * | 6 | 5357 | 2330.0 / 650.0 | 8050.0 / 265.0 |
 *
 * For all four inverses, OpenMP \f$8\f$-thread host runs and CUDA device runs match single-thread
 * gcc accuracy on both \f$x\f$ and \f$1 - x\f$.
 *
 * | Function | Suite | 99% ulpC (x / 1-x) | Max ulpC (x / 1-x) | Test bound (x / 1-x) |
 * | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
 * | `ibeta_inv()` | bulk | 4.09 / 7.27 | 7.05 / 19.5 | 750.0 / 4300.0 |
 * | `ibeta_inv()` | tail | 2.07 / 2.71 | 5.38 / 11.3 | 1250.0 / 800.0 |
 * | `ibetac_inv()` | bulk | 5.53 / 4.32 | 12.3 / 7.96 | 7000.0 / 230.0 |
 * | `ibetac_inv()` | tail | 1.83 / 2.75 | 4.40 / 4.39 | 2050.0 / 1400.0 |
 * | `ibeta_inv_logp()` | combined | 4.90 / 4.40 | 18.6 / 6.49 | 2000.0 / 1000.0 |
 * | `ibetac_inv_logq()` | combined | 3.28 / 5.34 | 5.82 / 16.3 | 1800.0 / 2450.0 |
 *
 * See \c docs/numerics.md for the test-suite sizes, parameter grids, and full error distributions.
 *
 * ## Speed
 *
 * On single-thread gcc with Laplace's own elementary functions, `beta_inc()` averages \f$263\f$ ns
 * per call on general points, \f$264\f$ ns on hard points, and \f$344\f$ ns on extreme points;
 * using the platform math library gives \f$370\f$ ns, \f$401\f$ ns, and \f$424\f$ ns across the
 * same three regimes, and all scalar value and log wrappers share that single-call cost. Across
 * orders \f$1\f$ through \f$5\f$, `log_ibeta_d1()` to `log_ibeta_d5()` take \f$1740\f$ to
 * \f$2970\f$ ns per call on single-thread gcc and \f$235\f$ to \f$397\f$ ns with \f$8\f$ OpenMP
 * threads, while `log_ibetac_d1()` to `log_ibetac_d5()` take \f$1420\f$ to \f$2430\f$ ns and
 * \f$250\f$ to \f$519\f$ ns. On an A100-SXM4-40GB over \f$10^6\f$ evaluations, shape derivatives
 * take \f$37\f$ to \f$140\f$ ns on mixed grids and \f$98\f$ to \f$495\f$ ns on homogeneous
 * deep-tail quadrature grids. Across the four inverse functions on single-thread gcc, a call
 * averages \f$976\f$ to \f$1303\f$ ns with Laplace's elementary functions and \f$1506\f$ to
 * \f$1979\f$ ns with the platform math library.
 *
 * ## Example
 *
 * \code{.cpp}
 * #include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>
 * #include <laplace/math/incomplete_beta.hpp>
 * #include <laplace/math/incomplete_beta_deriv.hpp>
 * #include <laplace/math/incomplete_inverse.hpp>
 *
 * void eval_beta(int n, const double* a, const double* b, const double* x,
 *                double* p_out, double* lp_out, double* da_out, double* xinv_out) {
 *     Kokkos::parallel_for(n, KOKKOS_LAMBDA(int i) {
 *         double p = laplace::math::ibeta(a[i], b[i], x[i]);
 *         p_out[i] = p;
 *         lp_out[i] = laplace::math::log_ibeta(a[i], b[i], x[i]);
 *         da_out[i] = laplace::math::log_ibeta_d1(a[i], b[i], x[i]).c[0];
 *         xinv_out[i] = laplace::math::ibeta_inv(a[i], b[i], p);
 *     });
 * }
 * \endcode
 *
